---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { id: 'getting-started-with-astro' } },
    { params: { id: 'react-best-practices' } },
    { params: { id: 'tailwind-css-tips' } },
  ];
}

const { id } = Astro.params;

const posts = {
  'getting-started-with-astro': {
    title: 'Getting Started with Astro',
    date: 'March 15, 2024',
    readTime: '5 min read',
    content: `
      <p>Astro is a modern web framework that helps you build fast, content-focused websites. In this post, we will explore the key features of Astro and how to get started with it.</p>
      
      <h2>What is Astro?</h2>
      <p>Astro is a web framework that focuses on delivering fast websites by shipping less JavaScript. It uses a unique island architecture where only the interactive components need JavaScript.</p>
      
      <h2>Key Features</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Island Architecture</li>
        <li>Zero JavaScript by default</li>
        <li>Supports multiple UI frameworks</li>
        <li>Edge-ready deployment</li>
      </ul>
      
      <h2>Getting Started</h2>
      <p>To create a new Astro project, run:</p>
      <pre><code>npm create astro@latest</code></pre>
      
      <p>This will guide you through the setup process and create a new Astro project with your preferred configuration.</p>
    `,
  },
  'react-best-practices': {
    title: 'React Best Practices in 2024',
    date: 'March 10, 2024',
    readTime: '8 min read',
    content: `
      <p>React continues to evolve, and staying up to date with best practices is crucial for building maintainable applications. Here are the key practices you should follow in 2024.</p>
      
      <h2>Component Design</h2>
      <p>Keep components small and focused. Each component should have a single responsibility and be reusable across your application.</p>
      
      <h2>State Management</h2>
      <p>Use React hooks for local state and consider libraries like Zustand or Redux Toolkit for complex state management needs.</p>
      
      <h2>Performance Optimization</h2>
      <p>Implement code splitting, lazy loading, and memoization to ensure your React applications remain fast and responsive.</p>
    `,
  },
  'tailwind-css-tips': {
    title: 'Tailwind CSS Tips and Tricks',
    date: 'March 5, 2024',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    content: `
      <p>Tailwind CSS is a utility-first CSS framework that provides low-level utility classes to build custom designs. Here are some tips to master Tailwind CSS.</p>
      
      <h2>Custom Configuration</h2>
      <p>Extend Tailwind is default theme in your tailwind.config.js file to add custom colors, spacing, and more.</p>
      
      <h2>Component Classes</h2>
      <p>Use the @apply directive to create reusable component classes while maintaining the utility-first approach.</p>
      
      <h2>Responsive Design</h2>
      <p>Master Tailwind is responsive prefixes (sm:, md:, lg:, xl:) to create truly responsive designs.</p>
    `,
  },
};

const post = posts[id as keyof typeof posts];
if (!post) {
  return Astro.redirect('/404');
}
---

<Layout title={post.title + ' | Blog'}>
  <Header />

  <main class="min-h-screen py-20 px-4">
    <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mb-4 text-green-primary">
          {post.title}
        </h1>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-gray-dim">
          <span>{post.date}</span>
          <span>•</span>
          <span>{post.readTime}</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="bg-gray-medium rounded-lg p-8 shadow-soft">
        <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-gray-dim">
          <div set:html={post.content} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <footer class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-light">
        <a
          href="/blog"
          class="text-green-primary hover:text-green-accent transition-colors"
        >
          ← Back to Blog
        </a>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
